  on the code.

  [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m currently logs the username on every login attempt, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m receives the raw password. Once logs ship to
  a third party, anything we log is retained on someone else's infrastructure — so what we send becomes a
  data-protection question, not a debugging convenience. The password never goes, on any option; that isn't a choice.
  The fork is the username and anything else user-identifying.

  - [1mStrict allowlist[0m — the logger sends only fields explicitly marked safe (event name, level, error type, timing, a
    random session id). Anything not on the list is dropped, including by accident. Buys the strongest guarantee and
    makes a leak require a deliberate code change. Costs some debugging power: you can see [3mthat[0m a login failed and
    correlate it to a session, but not [3mwhose[0m.
  - [1mAllowlist plus a hashed user id[0m — same, but the username goes through a one-way hash so you can group a single
    user's events and match them to a support report without storing the identifier itself.
  - [1mSend usernames in the clear[0m — most convenient for matching a report to a log line; puts user identifiers in a
    vendor's store and pulls the whole system into your GDPR/DPA scope.

  Hard to reverse: whatever reaches the vendor is already retained, and deleting it later means a vendor deletion
  request rather than a code change. Cheap to reverse: tightening the allowlist afterward, since it's one table in our
  wrapper.

  My recommendation is the allowlist plus hashed user id. It answers the actual support question — "is this one user or
  everyone, and which session" — without ever putting an identifier in the log store, and it means [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m's current
  plaintext username log goes away as part of this work rather than getting shipped to a vendor.
